00:03:54.339 --> 00:03:59.329
Frozen 2 pokes fun at a particularly iconic
moment from the first movie.

00:03:59.329 --> 00:04:04.620
After Elsa's "Show Yourself" song, Ahtohallan
reveals memories to her of past events including

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.419
childhood games with Anna and the Duke of
Weselton dancing.

00:04:08.419 --> 00:04:13.590
But when she sees the memory of herself strutting
her stuff during Let It Go, it makes her visibly

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:14.590
cringe.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:18.840
It's an amusing moment of Elsa feeling embarrassed
at the actions of her younger self, something

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.430
many adults will be able to relate to.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:24.920
But there's a second level to this joke as
not only is it about having outgrown your

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.350
younger self, it may also be a wink to those
in the audience who saw or heard that song

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.940
so many times it ended up driving them crazy!

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:38.110
That same moment from Let It Go was also parodied
earlier in the sequel when Olaf imitated Elsa's

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.960
signature self-assured sashay during a game
of charades.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.190
"Ooh, Elsa!"

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.840
The sequel also remedies complaints from fans
that Kristoff never got a full song of his

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.850
own in the first movie.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:54.410
"Reindeers are better than people.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:59.290
Sven, why is love so hard?"

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:04.190
This time Kristoff gets to belt out his very
own love song called Lost In The Woods.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:08.340
It's all about his struggles to move forward
in his relationship with Anna and there's

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:12.130
plenty about this new musical number that's
aimed specifically at adults.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:17.290
There's the whole retro style of the song,
which is basically a take on 80s power ballads.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.290
According to Kristen Anderson-Lopez, one half
of the husband-and-wife song-writing team

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:26.730
behind Frozen's mega hits, 'The 1980s was
that brief moment in music when men were allowed

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:31.080
to sing those big power ballads – and to
be vulnerable while doing it.'

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:35.100
Personally, when I watched the film, Lost
In The Woods had a strong feel of Peter Cetera

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:39.880
and Chicago's 80s power ballads, like Glory
of Love, the theme for The Karate Kid Part

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:43.770
2, and You're The Inspiration, which featured
in Deadpool.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.750
But the 80s vibe of Lost In The Woods doesn't
end with the music.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:53.300
Kristoff also gets the 80s music video treatment,
complete with music video wind for that signature

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.180
romantic breeze.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:59.070
Between the visuals and the song itself, the
Lost In The Woods sequence nicely balances

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:03.900
amusing images, perfect for a retro music
video, with Kristoff's very real sincerity

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.770
as he sings about his love for Anna.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.360
And speaking of classic music videos, it seems
the Frozen 2 team had one particularly famous

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.850
music video in mind when they were animating
Lost In The Woods.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.180
The way Sven and several other reindeer appear
as backing singers around Kristoff during

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:23.270
his new power ballad channels the look of
the ground-breaking promotional video that

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:27.800
Queen made in 1975 for their super-hit Bohemian
Rhapsody.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.610
The British rock band's video has been so
influential that it's credited with "practically

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:37.010
inventing the music video", and many adults
in the Frozen 2 audience will immediately

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:41.860
spot some of its signature style as Sven and
his fellow quadrupeds orbit Kristoff as they

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.590
provide reindeer harmonies for his lead vocals.

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:49.900
Fun fact: as well as voicing Kristoff, actor
Jonathan Groff also voices all the reindeer

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:50.900
in the song!

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.780
"You feel what you feel.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.389
And those feelings are real.

00:06:55.389 --> 00:07:01.090
Come on Kristoff, let down your guard."

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:05.170
When Elsa and Anna discover their parents'
shipwreck in the Enchanted Lands, they wonder

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.450
why and how their parents ended up there.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:12.170
Anna has the idea to look for answers in the
waterproof compartment that every Arendellian

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:13.170
ship has.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:18.139
Which prompts Olaf to muse, 'that's very clever;
although it does make me wonder why they don't

00:07:18.139 --> 00:07:20.030
just make the whole ship waterproof.'

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:25.870
This is actually a sly joke about aeroplanes
and their flight recorders, aka black boxes,

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:30.180
which are designed specifically to survive
airplane crashes so the flight data they store

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.390
can be used by accident investigators to determine
the cause of a crash.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:38.910
A fact which often leads people to joke: why
aren't aeroplanes made from the same material

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.650
as black boxes?!

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.840
One of the main themes that runs throughout
the film is change and how we adapt to it

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.970
and Olaf is a crucial character in getting
that point across.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.340
"How I wish this could last forever.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:50.340
Hmmm!

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.870
And yet change mocks us with her beauty."

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:57.260
Olaf was a bundle of innocence when we met
him in the first film, however now, instead

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:01.690
of dreaming up plans for summertime, Olaf
is trying to come to terms with the many changes

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:03.050
happening around him.

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.080
"Tell me, you're older and thus all-knowing.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.720
Do you ever worry about the notion that nothing
is permanent?"

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.840
What's especially amusing for adults in the
audience is that Olaf really believes that

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.780
everything he's going through and life in
general will all make sense when he's grown

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:17.780
up!

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:22.020
In fact, Olaf's song 'When I Am Older' includes
a lot of lines that adults will doubtless

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.990
find funny, such as:
'I'll have all the answers when I'm older';

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:31.490
'When I'm more mature, I'll feel totally secure',
and 'when you're older, absolutely everything

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:33.039
makes sense'.

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:37.899
Grown-up viewers will find the irony of Olaf's
song comical as they know getting older doesn't

00:08:37.899 --> 00:08:43.349
always mean you understand everything better,
sometimes things just seem even more ridiculous.

00:08:43.349 --> 00:08:45.499
"Well, you're the grown-ups you'd know."

00:08:45.499 --> 00:08:49.509
And adults will also laugh because they'll
probably remember they thought the same as

00:08:49.509 --> 00:08:52.110
Olaf when they were kids too!

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.260
In the three years between the events of the
two movies, Olaf has been reading and learning

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.339
a lot, and he drives the other characters
a little crazy on their journey north with

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:04.360
his fascinating facts that range from the
chances of getting struck by lightning to

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:05.490
the shape of wombat poop.

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:12.399
But the little snowman has even begun to ponder
some weightier, philosophical ideas.

00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:13.399
"Hey, let me ask you.

00:09:13.399 --> 00:09:17.579
How do you guys cope with ever-increasing
complexity of thought that comes with maturity?

00:09:17.579 --> 00:09:21.149
Oh, no, no, don't chew that, you don't know
what I've stepped in."

00:09:21.149 --> 00:09:25.439
In the Enchanted Forest, when Anna asks Olaf
for some help in understanding the mysterious

00:09:25.439 --> 00:09:31.080
ice sculptures created by Elsa's magic, just
before he gets to his "water has memory" explanation,

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.260
Olaf mentions his theory "about advancing
technologies [being] both our saviour and

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:37.260
our doom."

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:41.720
Many adults will find this reference both
humorous and true given the real-life benefits

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:46.220
and threats that stem from our ever-advancing
technological world, whether that's connecting

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:51.160
with friends and accessing information and
entertainment in just a few clicks, or actors

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:55.579
brought back from the dead to star in new
movies, driverless vehicles, and the rise

00:09:55.579 --> 00:09:57.449
of artificial intelligence.

00:09:57.449 --> 00:10:02.329
Perhaps Olaf's philosophical ponderings about
technology could even make a funny and informative

00:10:02.329 --> 00:10:07.290
short series, along the lines of Forky Asks
A Question, where Olaf learns, for example,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.579
how to stay safe online and protect his privacy.

